      ":question/id": "34924683-58db-5b74-a961-9f3350d7d885",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $-5x+2y=4?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $-5x+2y=4,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+2y & =4 \\\\ 2y & =5x+4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-\\frac{2}{5}x-7$ has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5})=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-\\frac{2}{5}x-7$ is perpendicular to the given line.",
