      ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $5x-3y=6?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $5x-3y=6,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-3y & =6 \\\\ -3y & =-5x+6 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{3}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-\\frac{3}{5}x+12$ has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{5})=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-\\frac{3}{5}x+12$ is perpendicular to the given line.",
