      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $-18x-16y=18?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $-18x-16y=18,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-18x-16y & =18 \\\\ -16y & =18x+18 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{18}{16}x-\\frac{18}{16} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{9}{8}x-\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{9}{8}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{8}{9}x-7$ has a slope of $\\frac{8}{9}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{9}{8})\\cdot \\frac{8}{9}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{8}{9}x-7$ is perpendicular to the given line.",
